// A Project AirSim drone flying over the M-City terrain in this plugin.
//
// "scene-type": "UnrealNative" is the important line. Project AirSim can load its own environment;
// this tells it NOT to, and to use whatever level is already open - which is how the drone ends up
// over this terrain instead of over its own.
//
// THE SPAWN POSITION IS THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG, and getting it wrong looks like the
// drone not existing. Project AirSim works in NED metres measured from the UNREAL WORLD ORIGIN:
//
//     north = Unreal X / 100        east = Unreal Y / 100        down = -Unreal Z / 100
//
// DOWN IS UNREAL Z, NOT ELEVATION. The landscape sits at Unreal Z 0, where Z 0 is 236.0 m above
// sea level (Tools/level.json, measured from the level). An earlier note here read an elevation
// off the heightmap and used it as the height, which put every start 236 m too high.
//
// The start below is at Unreal X=982700 Y=1095500 (cm). A trace straight down there hits the
// landscape at Z 2982 cm, and the scan is 8.45 m above that, so -291.0 starts the drone about
// 250 m above the scan. It stays there until the vertiport by the M-City outbuilding is placed;
// then the start goes on the pad. Pick a spot in the editor, convert it with the line above, and
// remember DOWN IS POSITIVE in NED.
{
  "id": "MCity",
  "actors": [
    {
      "type": "robot",
      "name": "Drone1",
      "origin": {
        "xyz": "9827.0 10955.0 -291.0",
        "rpy-deg": "0 0 0"
      },
      "robot-config": "robot_quadrotor_fastphysics_weather.jsonc"
    }
  ],
  "clock": {
    "type": "steppable",
    "step-ns": 3000000,
    "real-time-update-rate": 3000000,
    "pause-on-start": false
  },
  // M-City is in Ann Arbor, Michigan. This only sets the geodetic reference for lat/lon readouts and
  // gravity; the NED frame's origin is still the Unreal world origin.
  "home-geo-point": {
    "latitude": 42.29642,
    "longitude": -83.37792,
    "altitude": 250.0
  },
  // DELIBERATELY ZERO. Wind is expected to come from a per-robot wind provider in the sim when one
  // is present; anything set here is only the fallback when it is not. Leaving a value here would
  // make it unclear which of the two you were feeling.
  "wind": {
    "velocity": "0.0 0.0 0.0"
  },
  // SEGMENTATION IS WRITTEN INTO THE LEVEL, so Project AirSim's own pass is OFF. That pass makes
  // an id by adding up the letters of each mesh owner's name, overwrites whatever the level holds,
  // and reaches only what is loaded when the scene starts: every square kilometre of foliage would
  // be a different arbitrary class, and anything World Partition streams in later would have none.
  // The classes are Tools/segmentation.json, written into the components by
  // Tools/unreal/label_segmentation.py; the flight tools label the drone when the scene loads.
  "segmentation": {
    "initialize-ids": false,
    "ignore-existing": false,
    "use-owner-name": true
  },
  "scene-type": "UnrealNative"
}
